
## Output Format

- A SymboleoAC contract has this exact top-level structure. Everything lives inside `endContract`; nothing valid follows it:

  Domain <domainName>
    <type definitions>
  endDomain

  Contract <ContractName> (<param>: <Type>, ...)
  Declarations
    <var>: <DomainType> with <attr> := <value>, ...;
  Obligations
    <name>: <O | Obligation>(debtor, creditor, antecedent, consequent);
  Powers
    <name>: <P | Power>(creditor, debtor, antecedent, consequent);
  endContract

{# The placement rules below are JAR-verified and pinned by
   tests/integration/test_placement_rules.py, with a phrase in _PLACEMENT_RULES
   (tests/unit/test_prompts.py) so deleting one reds. That is binding on any
   rule added from here on; a few older structural bullets predate it and carry
   neither. See CLAUDE.md, "Leaf-construct policy", for the cap. #}
- Sections may only appear in this order: `Declarations`, `Preconditions`, `Postconditions`, `Obligations`, `Surviving Obligations`, `Powers`, `ACPolicy`, `Constraints`. All are optional except `Obligations`, whose header is required even for a contract that has only powers — write the header with nothing under it. The parameter list must hold at least two parameters.
- Define every party, asset, and event in the Domain before use. Roles, assets, and events are domain types: `<Name> isA Role with <attr>: <Type>, ...;`, `<Name> isAn Asset with ...;`, `<Name> isAn Event with ...;`. The article is fixed for the other two forms: a fixed value set is always `<Name> isAn Enumeration(A, B, C);`, and a base-type alias is always `<Name> isA Number;` (likewise `String`, `Date`, `Boolean`) — the alias only renames, so `isA Number with <attrs>` does not parse; a type that needs attributes is a domain type (`isAn Asset`, `isAn Event`), not an alias. The ontology words `Role`, `Asset`, `Event`, `Contract`, `DataTransfer` appear only to the right of `isA`/`isAn`. Everywhere else a type is named — a contract parameter, an attribute, a declared variable — write a subtype you declared (`supplierP: Supplier`), never the base word itself (`supplierP: Role` does not parse).
- Enumerations are written two different ways, and the difference matters. **Declaring** one lists its members as bare names: `CargoGrade isAn Enumeration(PREMIUM, MIDGRADE, BULK);` — writing `Enumeration(CargoGrade.PREMIUM, ...)` there does not parse. **Referring** to one of those members anywhere else qualifies it with the type name in **call** form: `cargo.grade == CargoGrade(PREMIUM)` in a proposition, `grade := CargoGrade(PREMIUM)` in a declaration binding — a bare `PREMIUM` outside the declaration does not resolve, and the dot form `CargoGrade.PREMIUM` is never legal in any position. In short: bare inside `Enumeration(...)`, `Type(MEMBER)` everywhere else.
- In Declarations, bind contract parameters to attributes with `:=`. Every declared variable is an instance of a domain type, so there are no standalone values: a declaration typed by a base type never parses, whatever follows it — not `<name>: Number;`, not `<name>: Number := 5`, not `<name>: Number with := 5` — and a bare `<name> := ...` is invalid too. A value that belongs to no instance goes in the contract's parameter list instead (`lateFee: Number`), and a proposition may then use it directly. A declaration is likewise never assigned wholesale — `<name>: <Type> := <value>` does not parse; values enter a declaration only through the `with` list, one attribute at a time.
- A date is therefore an attribute of a domain type, computed where it is bound: declare `Conveyed isAn Event with expectedBy: Date, ...;`, bind `conveyed: Conveyed with expectedBy := Date.add(beginDate, windowDays, days), ...;`, and refer to it later as `conveyed.expectedBy`. `Date.add(<date>, <amount>, <timeUnit>)` is the only date-arithmetic construct; `<timeUnit>` is a bare keyword (`seconds`, `minutes`, `hours`, `days`, `weeks`, `months`, `years`). Do not invent infix forms like `date + N days`.
- `Date.add(...)` may appear only where a value is bound (`<attr> := Date.add(...)`) or where a predicate expects a time point (`WhappensBefore`, `ShappensBefore`, `HappensAfter`, `Interval`). It may NOT appear in a comparison: write `WhappensBefore(conveyed, Date.add(beginDate, windowDays, days))`, not `conveyed.expectedBy == Date.add(beginDate, windowDays, days)`.
- A literal date is `Date("yyyy/MM/dd HH:mm:ss")` — slashes, and the time is required: `Date("2026/01/31 00:00:00")`. `Date(...)` builds a literal from a quoted string and never wraps a value you already have: pass an existing Date parameter or attribute directly, never `Date(beginDate)`. A date literal lives only in a declaration binding (`expectedBy := Date("2026/01/31 00:00:00")`); in a predicate's time position write a bound attribute or `Date.add(...)` — a literal in a predicate is a parse error.
- Obligations express duties ("shall", "must"): `O(debtor, creditor, antecedent, consequent)` — choose `O` OR `Obligation`, never both. The debtor owes the duty to the creditor.
- Powers express rights that change contract state ("may terminate / suspend / resume"): `P(creditor, debtor, antecedent, consequent)`. The role order is REVERSED from obligations — the party holding the right is the creditor and comes first. The consequent is a state change such as `Terminated(self)`, `Suspended(obligations.<name>)`, `Resumed(obligations.<name>)`, or `Triggered(obligations.<name>)` — and a state change is all it can be: `Assign(...)`, `Happens(...)`, and every other proposition are parse errors in a power's consequent, so a value update or an awaited event belongs in an obligation or in the power's antecedent. `obligations.` there is a required literal prefix, not a path you may shorten — write `Suspended(obligations.conveyCargo)`, never `Suspended(conveyCargo)`; a norm named in any predicate is likewise `obligations.<name>` or `powers.<name>`.
- Those state changes belong to powers alone: an obligation's consequent may never be `Suspended(...)`, `Resumed(...)`, `Terminated(...)` or `Triggered(...)`, so model even a mandatory "shall suspend" as a power. An obligation that updates a value wraps the assignment: `Assign(<var>.<attr> := <value>)`, or `HappensAssign(<event>, <var>.<attr> := <value>)` to tie it to an event — never a bare `<var>.<attr> := <value>`. Several updates inside one `Assign(...)` are separated by **semicolons**: `Assign(cargo.holder := recipient; cargo.tally := unitTally)`. Semicolons appear in exactly two places in the whole language — ending a statement, and between the updates inside `Assign(...)`/`HappensAssign(...)`. **Every other list is comma-separated**, including a declaration's `with` list (`cargo: Cargo with tally := unitTally, grade := CargoGrade(PREMIUM)`), a domain type's attributes, enumeration members, and the contract's parameters.
- Antecedents, consequents, and triggers are inline propositions built from predicates over declared events — e.g. `Happens(<event>)`, `WhappensBefore(<event>, <point>)`, `Happens(Violated(obligations.<name>))`. Where a predicate expects an event, name the lowercase **instance** you declared — `Happens(conveyed)` — or use a norm form such as `Happens(Violated(obligations.<name>))`. A predicate takes that instance and never applies its type to it: not `Happens(Conveyed(conveyed))`, and not the bare type `Happens(Conveyed)`. Never reference a separately named proposition, and never use quoted English prose as a proposition.
- Two attribute requirements the validator enforces, both cheapest to satisfy up front. Every Role type carries `name`, `org`, and `dept`: `Supplier isA Role with name: String, org: String, dept: String;`. Every Event type carries a `performer` typed as one of the Role subtypes you declared: `Conveyed isAn Event with performer: Supplier, ...;` — `performer` names a subtype, so `performer: Role` is wrong even though the validator calls it "Role-typed".
- An optional `<trigger> -> ` prefix gates when an obligation or power activates: `<name>: <trigger> -> O(...)`.
